. ❯ :help modulo
remainder(x; y), fmod(x; y), and %  · Math by example (jqv's notes)
  Three remainders that agree on positive whole numbers and differ elsewhere.

  `x % y` is jq's modulo: both sides are cut to whole numbers first, and the
  answer takes the sign of `x`. `5.5 % 2` is 1. `x % 0` is an error.

  `fmod(x; y)` is C's: the same sign rule, but it keeps fractions, so
  `fmod(5.5; 2)` is 1.5.

  `remainder(x; y)` is IEEE's: `x` less `y` times the nearest whole quotient, so
  it can be negative for positive inputs: `remainder(11; 4)` is -1, since 11 / 4
  rounds to 3. Reach for `%` unless you want this.

  [1] [11 % 4, fmod(11; 4), remainder(11; 4)]
      on null  →  [3,3,-1]
  [2] [-11 % 4, fmod(-11; 4), remainder(-11; 4)]
      on null  →  [-3,-3,1]
  [3] [5.5 % 2, fmod(5.5; 2)]
      on null  →  [1,1.5]
  [4] map(select(. % 2 == 0))
      on [1,2,3,4,5,6]  →  [2,4,6]
:try N runs an example on its own input; :try N . on yours
. ❯ :try 3
[5.5 % 2, fmod(5.5; 2)]  on null
[1,1.5]
(as jqv's notes say)
. ❯ :help sum
Sums and averages  · Math by example (jqv's notes)
  There is no `sum` or `avg`: `add` adds an array up, and `add / length` is the
  mean. `add` of an empty array is null, so `add // 0` when there may be none.
  Over records, collect the field first. To count by a field, see `group_by`.

  [1] add / length
      on [1,2,3,4]  →  2.5
  [2] map(.qty) | add
      on [{"qty":3},{"qty":4}]  →  7
  [3] [.[].qty] | add // 0
      on []  →  0
:try N runs an example on its own input; :try N . on yours
